// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// womb/kernel/trap/trap.vuma
// VWK Trap Dispatcher — routes traps by vector number (PMT-pure).
// Wave R2f — migrated onto the canonical superset TrapFrame (R2a).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// This file is the arch-independent trap dispatcher. After trap_entry (R2b/
// R2c/R2d's per-arch trap_trampoline.vuma) saves the native save area and
// the per-arch trap_to_superset adapter translates it into the canonical
// superset TrapFrame (R2a's womb/kernel/trap/trap_frame.vuma — 576 bytes,
// 8 fields), control reaches trap_handler(tf) here. The dispatcher routes
// by `tf.vector` (the interrupt-vector / exception-cause field):
//
//     vector 0..31   → CPU exception  → trap_panic(tf)
//                                       (in real kernel: panic with msg;
//                                        K12 adds real panic; hosted: nop)
//     vector 128     → syscall (int 0x80 on x86_64, SVC #0 on aarch64,
//                       ECALL on riscv64 — the per-arch adapter normalizes
//                       all three into vector == 128 here) → trap_syscall(tf)
//                                       (R4c provides the real syscall
//                                        dispatch; hosted: nop)
//     everything else → IRQ → trap_irq(tf)
//                                       (irq.vuma provides the real dispatch
//                                        via the IRQ ring buffer; hosted: nop)
//
// The dispatcher uses ONLY superset TrapFrame fields (tf.vector in the
// router; tf.pc / tf.sp / tf.error_code / tf.status / tf.gpr / tf.fpu are
// available for the per-handler consumers trap_panic / trap_syscall /
// trap_irq to read but are NOT touched by the router itself). No arch-
// specific field names like `tf.rax` (x86_64) or `tf.x0` (aarch64) or
// `tf.ra` (riscv64) appear anywhere in this file — the dispatcher is
// genuinely arch-independent, the R2 Golden Rule in TASKS.md ("Do not
// write arch-conditional logic inside kernel transforms").
//
// ── Why is the layout re-declared byte-identically to R2a? ──────────────────
// VUMA has no `import` statement (Open Work §7), so this file re-declares
// the canonical superset TrapFrame + tf_get_gpr + tf_set_gpr + tf_init
// byte-identically to womb/kernel/trap/trap_frame.vuma (R2a). The same
// byte-identical-re-declaration invariant that R2b/R2c/R2d's per-arch
// trap_trampoline.vuma files follow extends here: a mismatch in field
// order or type would silently produce different field offsets at the
// call site, so the layout + 3 helpers are copied verbatim from R2a's
// canonical file. Verified via the `awk + sort | uniq -c` cross-file
// invariant check after this wave lands (the worklog's Stage Summary
// records the result).
//
// ── Why is trap_panic a stub? ───────────────────────────────────────────────
// trap_panic is the CPU-exception path: in real kernel it would print a
// panic message with the trap info (vector, error_code, pc) and halt.
// K12 (the panic/log wave) adds real panic; until then, trap_panic is a
// no-op stub (the contract says "stub — K12 provides real panic"). Hosted
// mode never delivers real CPU exceptions (the host kernel does), so the
// stub is never exercised from the self-test except as a "did we crash?"
// smoke check.
//
// ── Why is trap_syscall a stub? ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// trap_syscall is the syscall-path entry point: in real kernel it reads
// the syscall number from tf (canonical GPR slot 5 = rdi on x86_64, x8 on
// aarch64, a7 on riscv64 — the per-arch adapter sets it), indexes into a
// syscall table, calls the handler with args 0..5 from GPR slots, and
// writes the return value back to GPR slot 0. R4c will provide the
// syscall table + dispatch logic. For now, trap_syscall is a no-op stub
// (the contract says "stub — R4c provides real dispatch"). Same "not a
// violation" rationale as trap_panic.
//
// ── Why is trap_irq a stub? ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
// trap_irq is the IRQ-path entry point: in real kernel it would call
// irq_dispatch_loop from the sibling irq.vuma module — popping vectors
// off the per-CPU IrqRing and dispatching each. But VUMA has no `import`
// and the dispatcher doesn't have an IrqRing in hand (the IrqRing is a
// per-CPU global allocated by kmain and passed to irq_dispatch_loop
// explicitly). In hosted mode, trap_irq is a no-op (the host kernel
// handles hardware IRQs — VUMA code never sees them). In bare-metal K11,
// trap.S will load the per-CPU IrqRing + IrqTable pointers into RDI/RSI
// before calling trap_irq, OR trap_irq will receive them as additional
// State<T> parameters (K11 will pick one). For now, trap_irq is a no-op
// stub (the contract says "stub — irq.vuma provides real dispatch"). The
// real IRQ dispatch loop is in irq.vuma's irq_dispatch_loop (this wave
// R2f) and is exercised by irq.vuma's self-test.
//
// ── Why nested ifs instead of else-if? ──────────────────────────────────────
// VUMA's if-expression syntax (K2a) supports `if cond { ... } else { ... }`
// but not `else if` chained. The dispatcher therefore uses nested ifs:
//     if v < 32 { panic } else { if v == 128 { syscall } else { irq } }
// This is the same nested-if pattern K3c's trap_is_page_fault uses for
// its range check (12 <= scause <= 15 → nested-if form) and the same
// shape K3d's pre-R2f trap.vuma used before this wave migrated it onto
// the superset.
//
// ── PMT discipline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NO `*ptr`, `&x`, `allocate`, or `free` anywhere in this file. The
// TrapFrame is a 576-byte flat typed-memory record held in a
// `___pmt_buffer`-backed State<TrapFrame>; the CALLER allocates it via
// `state_new(TrapFrame)` and passes it by reference to `tf_init` /
// `trap_handler` / `trap_panic` / `trap_syscall` / `trap_irq` (init-style
// API — see "Why init-style?" in womb/kernel/mm/pmm.vuma for the codegen
// rationale). No externs are declared in this file (the dispatcher and
// its sub-handlers don't directly touch the PIC or CR2 — those are
// R2b/R2c/R2d's trap_trampoline.vuma's externs, used by the per-arch
// adapter and by the K3e/K11 page-fault handler). The sub-handlers
// (trap_panic, trap_syscall, trap_irq) take State<TrapFrame> by regular
// VUMA-fn borrow (NOT an extern #[borrow] — VUMA fn calls don't need
// the attribute, only extern "C" calls do, because the codegen tracks
// the State's lifetime through regular fn calls but conservatively
// invalidates on extern calls). None of the sub-handlers call any
// extern, so there is no use-after-invalidate risk.
//
// ── VUMA has no `import` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// This file is self-contained. It re-declares the canonical superset
// TrapFrame + tf_get_gpr + tf_set_gpr + tf_init byte-identically to R2a's
// trap_frame.vuma (the self-test needs a zeroed TrapFrame to test vector
// routing, and tf_init is the canonical way to zero one — same pattern
// as R2a's own self-test). The sibling irq.vuma module (also R2f-updated)
// re-declares the IrqRing layout + helpers byte-identically to R2e's
// irq_ring.vuma; this file does NOT re-declare IrqRing because trap_irq
// is a no-op stub (when K11 removes the stub, K11 will re-declare IrqRing
// in this file or pass it through the call chain from kmain).
//
// ── Decimal-constants discipline ────────────────────────────────────────────
// All non-zero constants in this file use DECIMAL per the R2f contract's
// "Use decimal constants" rule. Decimal avoids the parser's hex-literal
// path (parse_int_radix width-extension subtleties — see
// womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for the same rationale, and
// R2a's trap_frame.vuma for the same rule applied to vector=14 and
// pc=4096). The constants:
//     32  = 0x20  boundary between CPU exceptions (0..31) and IRQs
//                 (32..N) — the 8259 PIC's IRQs are remapped to vectors
//                 32..47 by the IDT setup in K11's boot path on x86_64;
//                 on aarch64/riscv64 the value 32 is just a "high enough"
//                 threshold that distinguishes CPU exception ECs (0..63
//                 on aarch64, but the real IRQs go through the dedicated
//                 irq.vuma path, not this dispatcher) from the syscall
//                 sentinel (128).
//     128 = 0x80  the int 0x80 syscall vector on x86_64 (Linux's legacy
//                 32-bit syscall ABI; modern Linux uses SYSCALL/SYSRET
//                 but the VUMA kernel follows the simpler int 0x80
//                 convention for portability across all three arches —
//                 aarch64 uses SVC #0, riscv64 uses ECALL from U-mode;
//                 the per-arch adapter normalizes all three into
//                 vector == 128 here so the dispatcher has a single
//                 syscall-detection check).
//     0   = self-test vector 0 (CPU exception — lowest valid exception
//                 vector on all arches)
//     256, 8, 24, 255, 0  = layout + helper constants (same as R2a)
//
// ── Build & verify (hosted x86_64) ──────────────────────────────────────────
//     . "$HOME/.cargo/env" && CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 \
//         cargo build --profile release-fast --bin compile_dump
//     ./target/release-fast/compile_dump \
//         womb/kernel/trap/trap.vuma \
//         /tmp/trap.bin x86_64 --verify
//     /tmp/trap.bin ; echo "exit=$?"
// Expected: IVE Pass, exit=0 (the 3 self-test dispatches all succeed
// without crashing — vector 0 → trap_panic, vector 128 → trap_syscall,
// vector 32 → trap_irq).

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Layout — Canonical superset TrapFrame (re-declared byte-identically to
// R2a's womb/kernel/trap/trap_frame.vuma; 576 bytes total).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The 8-field superset layout. Re-declared verbatim from R2a (same field
// names, same field types, same field order, same _pad size) per the
// byte-identical-re-declaration invariant. See R2a's trap_frame.vuma
// header for the full field semantics + per-arch canonical GPR slot
// mapping (x86_64/aarch64/riscv64).

layout TrapFrame = {
    gpr: [u8; 256],        // 32 × 8 bytes — general-purpose registers (LE)
    pc: u64,               // program counter (rip / elr_el1 / mepc)
    status: u64,           // status register (rflags / spsr_el1 / mstatus)
    vector: u64,           // interrupt vector / exception cause
    error_code: u64,       // CPU error code / esr_el1 / stval
    sp: u64,               // stack pointer at trap entry
    fpu: [u8; 256],        // 32 × 8 bytes — FPU/SIMD registers (optional, LE)
    _pad: [u8; 24],        // alignment padding (zeroed by tf_init)
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Helpers (re-declared byte-identically to R2a's trap_frame.vuma).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// tf_get_gpr / tf_set_gpr / tf_init — copied verbatim from R2a so this
// file can be compiled standalone (no `import`). The self-test calls
// tf_init to zero a fresh TrapFrame before exercising the dispatcher.

// Read the u64 GPR at canonical slot `idx` from tf.gpr. 8-byte LE unpack
// at byte offset `idx * 8`. Same loop pattern as task.vuma's pt_get_pid
// but with a u64 accumulator and 8-byte loop bound. No `*ptr` / `&tf` /
// `allocate` anywhere.
fn tf_get_gpr(tf: State<TrapFrame>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tf.gpr[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 `val` to canonical slot `idx` in tf.gpr. 8-byte LE pack
// at byte offset `idx * 8`. Same loop pattern as task.vuma's pt_set_pid
// but with 8-byte loop bound + u64 mask. No `*ptr` / `&tf` / `allocate`.
fn tf_set_gpr(tf: State<TrapFrame>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tf.gpr[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// Zero every field of a caller-allocated TrapFrame. Called once per
// TrapFrame before any arch-specific trap_to_superset adapter (R2b/R2c/
// R2d) populates the arch-specific fields. Explicit byte-zeroing loops
// for gpr/fpu/_pad (not relying on state_new's zero-fill) — same pattern
// as R1a's bootinfo_init. No `*ptr` / `&tf` / `allocate` anywhere.
fn tf_init(tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    let i = 0;
    while i < 256 { tf.gpr[i] = 0; i = i + 1; }
    tf.pc = 0;
    tf.status = 0;
    tf.vector = 0;
    tf.error_code = 0;
    tf.sp = 0;
    let j = 0;
    while j < 256 { tf.fpu[j] = 0; j = j + 1; }
    let k = 0;
    while k < 24 { tf._pad[k] = 0; k = k + 1; }
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Trap dispatcher.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Routes by `tf.vector`. The three branches are documented in the file
// header. The nested-if structure (instead of else-if) is required by
// VUMA's if-expression syntax (K2a — see "Why nested ifs" above). `tf`
// is passed by regular VUMA-fn borrow to all sub-handlers; none of the
// sub-handlers call any extern, so the State's lifetime is preserved
// across the calls (the codegen's conservative invalidation only fires
// on extern "C" calls).
//
// The dispatcher reads ONLY tf.vector in the router itself. The sub-
// handlers may read other fields (tf.pc, tf.sp, tf.error_code, etc.) but
// all such reads are through the superset field names — NO arch-specific
// field names like tf.rax / tf.x0 / tf.ra appear anywhere. This is the
// R2 Golden Rule in TASKS.md ("Do not write arch-conditional logic
// inside kernel transforms").

// Trap dispatcher: routes by vector number.
//   vector 0..31   → CPU exception → trap_panic
//   vector 128     → syscall       → trap_syscall
//   everything else → IRQ          → trap_irq
fn trap_handler(tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    let v = tf.vector;
    if v < 32 {
        // CPU exception — panic path.
        trap_panic(tf);
    } else {
        if v == 128 {
            // Syscall (int 0x80 on x86_64 / SVC #0 on aarch64 / ECALL on
            // riscv64 — normalized to vector == 128 by the per-arch
            // adapter).
            trap_syscall(tf);
        } else {
            // Hardware IRQ.
            trap_irq(tf);
        }
    }
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Per-vector-class sub-handlers (hosted-mode stubs — R4c/K11/K12 fill in).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// CPU exception handler — just return (in real kernel: panic with msg).
// K12 adds the real panic path (print the trap frame + halt). Hosted
// mode never delivers real CPU exceptions, so this stub is never
// exercised from the self-test except as a "did we crash?" check.
fn trap_panic(tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    // In hosted mode, just return. K12 adds real panic.
    return;
}

// Syscall handler stub — R4c provides the real implementation.
// R4c will read the syscall number from GPR slot (per-arch: rdi on x86_64,
// x8 on aarch64, a7 on riscv64 — the per-arch adapter sets it), index
// into a syscall table, call the handler with args 0..5 from GPR slots,
// and write the return value back to GPR slot 0. Hosted mode never
// delivers syscalls via the trap path (the host kernel uses its own
// syscall ABI), so this stub is never exercised from the self-test
// except as a "did we crash?" check.
fn trap_syscall(tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    // In hosted mode, just return.
    return;
}

// IRQ handler stub — irq.vuma provides real dispatch via irq_dispatch_loop.
// In real kernel (K11), trap_irq would:
//   1. Read tf.vector
//   2. Look up the per-CPU IrqRing + IrqTable (passed by kmain or loaded
//      from a per-CPU control block)
//   3. Call irq_dispatch_loop(ring, tbl) from the sibling irq.vuma module
//      (R2f) — popping vectors off the ring and dispatching each.
// For now, just return (stub). K11 removes the stub.
fn trap_irq(tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    // R2f irq.vuma provides irq_dispatch_loop.
    // For now, just return (stub).
    return;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Self-test.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Allocate a TrapFrame via state_new, zero it via tf_init, then exercise
// all three dispatcher branches:
//   (1) vector 0   → trap_panic path (CPU exception)
//   (2) vector 128 → trap_syscall path (int 0x80 / SVC #0 / ECALL)
//   (3) vector 32  → trap_irq path (the first non-exception, non-syscall
//                    vector — on x86_64 this is the timer IRQ, the first
//                    PIC vector after the 8259 PIC's IRQ-base remap)
// Each dispatch is a "did we crash?" smoke check — the hosted-mode sub-
// handlers are all no-ops, so the only failure mode is a codegen/IVE
// issue (e.g. invalid State<TrapFrame> access, malformed if-expression
// lowering). If all three dispatches complete without crashing, main
// returns 0.
//
// Per the R2f contract's "Use decimal constants" rule: all non-zero
// constants in the self-test use DECIMAL (32, 128) — decimal avoids the
// parser's hex-literal path (parse_int_radix width-extension subtleties
// — see womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for the same rationale,
// and R2a's trap_frame.vuma for the same rule applied to vector=14 and
// pc=4096).

fn main() -> i32 {
    let tf = state_new(TrapFrame);
    tf_init(tf);

    // Test 1: vector 0 (CPU exception) → trap_panic (no crash).
    tf.vector = 0;
    trap_handler(tf);

    // Test 2: vector 128 (syscall) → trap_syscall (no crash).
    tf.vector = 128;
    trap_handler(tf);

    // Test 3: vector 32 (IRQ) → trap_irq (no crash).
    tf.vector = 32;
    trap_handler(tf);

    return 0;
}
